H2: White Label vs Grey Label
If you're comparing options, you'll quickly come across the grey label model as well. Both put your brand in front of clients, but they're built differently underneath.
H3: White Label
 A white label generally gives you greater control over the platform and its configuration. You have more freedom to shape trading conditions, account structures and how the environment is run. It involves a larger investment and more operational responsibility, but it gives your business a stronger, more independent foundation.
H3: Grey Label
 A grey label operates under another provider's infrastructure and licensing arrangement. You get your own branded space within that provider's environment, with lower entry costs and less to manage. The trade-off is less flexibility and more reliance on the host provider.
H3: How to Decide
 Think about where you want the business to be in two or three years, not just on launch day. If you expect meaningful volumes, want to shape your own trading conditions or plan to expand into new products and regions, a white label usually makes more sense. If you're testing a market with a tight budget, a grey label can be a practical first step, and our MT5 grey label page goes into that model in detail.
